At terminal velocity, the net force on the object is zero. The downward weight (W) is balanced by the upward buoyancy (U) and upward drag (D).
Equation: W = U + D

1.  Calculate Buoyancy (U):
    U = \rho_{fluid} \times V \times g
    U = 1500 \text{ kg m}^{-3} \times 0.20 \text{ m}^3 \times 10 \text{ m s}^{-2}
    U = 3000 \text{ N}

2.  Substitute U and D into the equilibrium equation to find W:
    W = 3000 \text{ N} + 1000 \text{ N}
    W = 4000 \text{ N}

3.  Use the definition of weight (W = \rho_{obj} \times V \times g) to find \rho_{obj}:
    4000 \text{ N} = \rho_{obj} \times 0.20 \text{ m}^3 \times 10 \text{ m s}^{-2}
    4000 = \rho_{obj} \times 2
    \rho_{obj} = \frac{4000}{2}
    \rho_{obj} = 2000 \text{ kg m}^{-3}
